{
  "image_name": "1f3fa5eb3d6a052a10a81382d307e413.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes major RNA fluorescence in situ hybridization strategies used to detect single transcripts and sequence variants in cells. Overall, it shows how many fluorescently labeled probes bind along an RNA to generate bright punctate smFISH signals, and how different probe designs increase sensitivity, multiplexing, or specificity.\n\nOne part compares conventional probe tiling, where multiple probes span a transcript, with more densely spaced shorter probes. Another section illustrates three coding schemes for identifying many RNAs at once: spatial barcoding, where colored probe sets occupy different positions on a transcript; spectral barcoding, where different dye combinations encode targets; and sequential barcoding, where probes are removed and rehybridized over multiple imaging rounds so the same transcript changes color over time.\n\nA larger schematic depicts a variant-detection approach centered on an SNV. It appears to use an allele-specific probe and a branched amplification cascade involving pre-amplifiers and amplifiers, creating many dye molecules around a single binding event to boost the signal while preserving sequence specificity.\n\nThe final panel shows a close-up of probe architecture: paired primary probes bind adjacent regions on the RNA and provide a landing site for amplification. Visible spacing labels indicate approximate nucleotide distances needed for the probe pair to assemble properly.",
